y by chromaffin cells in the adrenal medulla and the adrenal glands. They include key hormones such as adrenaline, noradrenaline, and dopamine. These hormones play a crucial role in the body's stress response, influencing various physiological processes, including lipid metabolism and the metabolic state of critically ill patients. \n\nCatecholamines are particularly significant in the context of certain medical conditions. Their hypersecretion is a characteristic feature of pheochromocytomas, which are tumors of the adrenal glands that can lead to symptoms such as hypertension. The measurement of catecholamines is often utilized in the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma. Additionally, catecholamines are released in response to hypovolemia and sympathetic stimulation, especially in patients who have suffered burns, further impacting their metabolism. They are also released during stress and can affect the metabolic state in patients with acute pancreatitis. Overall, catecholamines are vital hormones that mediate the body's response to stress and various pathological conditions.\",13\r\n14164,VAMA,\"Vanilmandelic acid (VAMA) is a test used to diagnose tumors derived from chromaffin cells, such as pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas.\",2\r\n5255,ATOVASTATINA,Atorvastatin is a medication that can cause false positives in the measurement of catecholamines and metanephrines.,1\r\n5257,COCAÍNA,\"Cocaine, known as \"\"cocaína\"\" in Spanish, is a drug that can cause elevated levels of catecholamines and metanephrines in the body. This elevation can result in false positives during medical tests, particularly complicating the diagnosis of pheochromocytomas, which are tumors that can affect catecholamine levels. The presence of cocaine in the system can therefore interfere with accurate medical assessments and diagnoses.\",1\r\n14167,ÁCIDOS HOMOVANÍLICO Y VANILMANDÉLICO,Homovanillic acid and vanilmandelic acid are metabolites of catecholamines used in the diagnosis of neuroendoc